  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nAs of 1pm the bulk of the rain has moved out of the CWA with clouds \ndiminishing west to east across the CWA besides fair weather \ncumulus. Temps range in the upper 60s to the lower 80s with \ndewpoints in the upper 50s to the upper 60s. The shortwave and its \nassociated surface low will continue to exit the region as it tracks \nsoutheast this evening and tonight. Behind the shortwave, winds \naloft remain out of the north and northwest. An amplified ridge \ncontinues over the western CONUS with its large mid level high \nunderneath. Through the weekend models have a good handle on the \nridge becoming positive tilted, with its axis extending from central \nCanada and southwestward through the Northern Plains through the \nRockies. This is due to a large shortwave/low that will track \neastward then northeastward across Canada and phasing with another \nshortwave. By early next week, the axis of the ongoing positive \ntilted ridge shifts eastward with the Northern Plains on the \nsouthern extent of this trough as winds turn zonal to a slight \nsouthwesterly component. Models agree well on track and timing, \nhowever, a bit of a difference in intensity and surface low. Well \ninto the extended, behind the shortwave, a broad low amplitude ridge \nwill develop over the western two thirds of the CONUS. Again, due to \nthis being further out in time, models differ on how fast the \nshortwave moves out and exact amplitude of the broad ridge, so low \nconfidence on this as of now.\n\nWith this setup, dry conditions are expected through the weekend \nwhich will help locations that received several inches of rain \nyesterday dry out. Precip chances return Monday/Tuesday as this \nshortwave's surface low tracks across central Canada with its \nassociated cold front tracking southeast across the Northern Plains. \nNBM's highest pops (as of now) is 40 to 85% Monday evening/night \nthrough Tuesday morning ahead and along the cold front with the \nhighest pops James valley and eastward through MN. PWAT ahead of the \nfront in the WAA area as it tracks southeast will range from ~1 to \n1.50\" but still a bit early on possible amounts. NBM prob of 0.25\" \nas of now is 35-45% east of the Coteau. Also still a bit early \nregarding chances of severe weather, however, ML guidance does \nindicate a 5% chance mainly east of the Missouri River Monday. SPC \nhas a 15% chance of severe weather over eastern ND/northwestern MN \nfor now. Details will be nailed down closer to time. \n\nWith the ridge aloft tilting over the CWA this weekend, warmer air \nwill move back in from the west aloft in combination with WAA 850mb \nto surface winds. This warmer air will be strongest mainly along and \nwest of the Missouri River Saturday, with highs jumping back up in \nthe upper 80s to the upper 90s here. This warmer air spreads \neastward to the James Valley Sunday with highs ranging in the 90s to \nthe lower 100s west of Brown/Spink Counties and hottest over north \ncentral SD. With dewpoints increasing per this stronger return flow \nSunday at the surface, they will increase back into the 60s to \npotentially lower 70s Sunday. Heat index values could be near or \naround 100 over portions of central SD with HeatRisk in the Moderate \nto High category along and west river. So a heat headline may be \nneeded closer to time. One more day of warm temps Monday (80s to \nlower 90s) before cooler air moves in behind the cold front Tuesday \nwith models hinting at highs only in the 70s! It does look like heat \nreturns and will gradually filter eastward by the end of next week \nwith highs back in the 80s/90s.\n\nLastly, waves of smoke aloft continue for the next several days \ncreating a hazy sky, however, less concentration of smoke are \nexpected at the surface through Saturday per HRRR. We will have to \nwatch the potential for elevated fire danger Sunday across north \ncentral and central SD as the above normal heat will combine with \nstrong southerly wind gusts (30-40 mph) and RH values dropping to \n30% or lower along and west of the Mo River. Grassland Fire Danger \nIndex in the High to Very High category for this area.",
